 "lp": -0.09620682895183563, "top": [[" more", -0.09620682895183563], [" the", -2.809366464614868]]}, {"t": " concrete", "lp": -0.2839401960372925, "top": [[" concrete", -0.2839401960372925], [" precise", -2.013278007507324]]}, {"t": " guidance", "lp": -0.4732844829559326, "top": [[" guidance", -0.4732844829559326], [",", -1.2245471477508545]]}, {"t": " for", "lp": -0.8675394058227539, "top": [[" for", -0.8675394058227539], [" than", -1.0937280654907227]]}, {"t": " your", "lp": -0.0020827867556363344, "top": [[" your", -0.0020827867556363344], [" the", -6.478994369506836]]}, {"t": " setup", "lp": -0.21044033765792847, "top": [[" setup", -0.21044033765792847], [" use", -2.3478972911834717]]}, {"t": ".", "lp": -0.0034748418256640434, "top": [[".", -0.0034748418256640434], [" specifically", -6.948050022125244]]}, {"t": "", "lp": -0.0066202785819768906, "top": [["", -0.0066202785819768906], ["\n\n", -5.032624244689941]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver will \"steal\" CCS contexts from other workloads when needed\n- More aggressive resource management\n- Can preempt or take over CCS resources from other contexts\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Dedicated allocation**: CCS contexts are allocated more statically/dedicated\n- Less aggressive stealing behavior\n- Generally more predictable but potentially less flexible\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- Typically represents an even more **restricted or pinned** allocation strategy\n- CCS resources are more firmly reserved\n\n---\n\n## When to use each\n\n**Important caveat**: The exact semantics of these modes have historically been somewhat undocumented and have changed across driver versions. The behavior isn't perfectly stable, so I'd recommend verifying against your specific driver version.\n\n**General guidance:**\n\n- **Default (usually mode 1)**: Fine for most use cases\n- **Mode 2**: May help if you're seeing instability or want more predictable CCS allocation\n- **Mode 4**: Consider if you're doing workloads that need guaranteed CCS isolation, or if you're debugging CCS-related issues\n\n---\n\n## Practical advice\n\n1. **Check your current driver version** \u2014 the semantics matter and may differ\n2. **Test empirically** \u2014 run your specific workload with different modes and measure\n3. **Check logs** \u2014 the Xe driver logs CCS allocation behavior which can be informative\n\nWould you like me to help you find your specific driver version or interpret driver logs for CCS allocation? That would give you more concrete guidance for your setup."}